WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.600
The Avengers have fought a lot of bad guys
over the years.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:08.200
But despite having a rogues' gallery as long
as your arm, only a handful of villains have

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.260
actually made their way to the big screen.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:15.500
And only one of them can even be said to truly
be an Avengers villain first and foremost:

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:16.400
Ultron.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:24.140
First appearing in 1968, Ultron has been resurrected
again, and again, and again, and again so

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:28.529
many times that you'd need a master's degree
in Marvelology to catalogue them all.

00:00:28.529 --> 00:00:32.870
The character's constant design changes means
the talented people in charge of adapting

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:38.309
him for 2015's Avengers: Age of Ultron had
their work cut out for them in terms of coming

00:00:38.309 --> 00:00:40.140
up with a great cinematic design.

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:45.670
Fortunately, Charlie Wen former head of visual
development at Marvel Studios was up to the

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:46.240
challenge.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:51.100
Wen sat for an exclusive interview to go over
all the ins and outs of designing the movie

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.340
version of the homicidal robot that Marvel
fans love to hate.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:01:00.820
When he first appeared in 1968's Avengers
#54, Ultron didn't have a name, much less

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.280
any distinct design.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:07.180
The issue's story concerns the Masters of
Evil teaming up to capture the Avengers with

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:10.660
the help of secrets provided by the team's
butler, Jarvis.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.120
By the story's end, one of the Masters of
Evil's newest members, the Crimson Cowl, is

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:20.240
revealed to be an unnamed robot decoy, covering
for the Cowl's true alter ego: Jarvis the

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:20.740
butler.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:25.440
Then, by the next issue, writer and artist
Roy Thomas and John Buscema [byoo-SEM-ah]

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.540
apparently realized that suddenly making Jarvis
evil for no actual reason wasn't the best

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:31.040
move.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:36.800
Instead made the random robot — now apparently
named Ultron 5, the Living Automaton — the

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:42.140
true villain all along, who hypnotized the
Avengers' faithful butler into temporary betrayal.

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:46.920
Even with the revelation of this new villain
named Ultron 5, readers still wouldn't learn

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:52.060
much about his actual origins for several
issues, specifically Avengers #58.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.580
There, it's revealed that Hank "Giant Man"
Pym was experimenting with creating "synthetic

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:02.060
life," and built a goofy robot that looked
more like the clanking boiler you might have

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:04.840
in your basement than the mechanical menace
he'd become.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:10.340
After his initial fight with Giant Man, Ultron
somehow erased his science-daddy's memory

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:13.640
and just jumped through a concrete wall like
the Kool-Aid man

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:18.420
, but with more lasers and

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.400
inexplicable memory-erasing technology.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:26.140
Among these first appearances, only a few
design elements managed to stick: some swooping

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:31.099
angled lines on Ultron's face that resemble
angry eyebrows, some extraneous metal bits

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:35.260
that kind of look like robot ears, and a mouth
full of chunky robo-teeth.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:41.060
Over time, Ultron's defining chompers somehow
morphed into a weird, toothy grin.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:45.740
As a result, Ultron looks pretty… well,
he looks pretty dumb, right?

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:50.460
Marvel Studios' former head of visual development
Charlie Wen, who led the team responsible

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:53.620
for adapting Ultron to the big screen, more
or less agreed:

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:58.000
"If you look at the comic book Ultron, he
looks very simple in the head.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:00.100
That's always the feel of it."

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:04.860
Interestingly, Wen tapped into that idea while
creating concept art for a sequence in the

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:08.480
film after Ultron's coated a new version of
himself in vibranium.

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:09.420
The reason?

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:14.720
Because even though Ultron looks "simple,"
he also looks deranged not a quality you want

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:17.820
to shy away from if you want to make your
villain as menacing as possible.

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:24.500
"We knew that Ultron from Joss Whedon's script
at that moment he was sort of going nuttier.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:25.920
Even the way he talks.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:30.260
Joss really wanted to get an Ultron that just
felt like he wasn't right in the head.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:33.440
That was trying to come up with a look for
that."

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:37.980
Diehard comic book fans may have been upset
that the classic look didn't make it into

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:39.620
the film on Ultron Prime.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:43.959
However, Wen and his team brought the old
school comic book robo-grin to the villain's

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:45.040
henchmen instead.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:50.260
The Ultron Sentries are the villain's mechanical
representatives who plague the Avengers while

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:53.340
they try to save Sokovia from complete annihilation.

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:58.960
Another reason Wen saved the classic Ultron
face for the sentries was simple practicality.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:04.260
Discussing the classic comic book look for
Ultron's face, Wen pointed out the difficulties

00:04:04.269 --> 00:04:06.989
that would come from trying to put it on your
main bad guy:

00:04:06.989 --> 00:04:10.200
"You're getting many design challenges here.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.160
One is how are you going to animate that face?"

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:15.460
Scrub to 4:56: "You're not going to get a
lot of expression from that either.

00:04:15.469 --> 00:04:16.469
He needs to talk.

00:04:16.469 --> 00:04:17.320
He needs to express."

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:21.820
So it's not surprising that the sentries got
the grin, rather than Ultron Prime.

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:27.560
The Ultron Sentries are basically canon fodder,
disposable bad guys the heroes can chew through

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:29.620
while trying to get to the real villain.

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:34.040
They didn't need to do much beyond fight and
die, meaning that they were the perfect vehicle

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:35.060
for the classic look.

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:40.260
Wen and his team had to find lots of new angles,
and explore tons of different ideas to bring

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:45.080
Ultron to the big screen including a much
more human-looking version of the mad robot

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:48.420
villain, which is on display in these three
side-by-side images.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:53.570
To explain his approach in these concept art
images, Wen referred back to some of Ultron's

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:56.200
most famous lines from his introduction in
the film:

00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:02.300
"I was tangled in...strings.

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:04.480
Had to kill the other guy."

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:07.500
"Character wise He's more like an evil Pinocchio.

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:09.140
He's like, 'I want to be alive.

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:10.140
I want to be a real boy.'

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:12.540
I mean Ultron wants to be a real person.

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:16.390
I mean he's becoming more and more real but
sort of his circuits are messed up and things

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:16.980
are getting crossed.

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:19.160
How do you get that across in his expressions?

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:23.800
We knew we needed to have a face that can
have subtle expression."

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:29.040
"[...] we did a lot of focus on just how those
plates can move, what can make it feel like

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:32.300
it's expressing in certain ways and how does
it form?

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:36.400
There's so much to try to figure out in that
and that's why it took such a long time.”

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:41.340
Ultimately, it's pretty tough to translate
a design like Ultron's to the movies, but

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:45.800
Wen worked to transform the character from
a goofy comic book robot to a dangerously

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:46.860
intelligent foe.

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:52.300
With that in mind, this concept image clearly
communicates the character's intelligence...and

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:53.450
lurking menace.

00:05:53.450 --> 00:05:56.280
"I really liked an elegant version of Ultron.

00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:01.060
I did some that I felt like were going more
towards that, but then in it he also needed

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:05.630
to really show sort of an instant power from
the first read.

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:07.000
We kind of had something in between.

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:10.460
Somewhat elegant but also showed enough presence
and power."

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:15.240
That sums up the balance Wen struck in the
end: a blend of power and presence that made

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:18.860
for one of the most unforgettable comic book
villains to hit the silver screen.

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:24.320
Thanos and his fancy glove may be all the
rage these days, but don't forget that Ultron

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:29.140
was nearly too much for the Avengers to handle…
and he didn't have a fancy magic glove he

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:31.780
could use to snap himself to victory, either.

